PRES.append({
 "name": "Self-care and independence skills at school",
 "neps": NEPS_16,
 "related_to": ["Developmental Coordination Disorder (dyspraxia)", "Intellectual Disability", "Autism", "Hypermobility spectrum disorder", "Physical disability (e.g., cerebral palsy, spina bifida — medical)"],
 "what_it_is": [
  "The practical skills a pupil needs to get through the school day without adult help: dressing and undressing for PE, fastenings and shoelaces, toileting and hand-washing, opening lunch containers and eating, managing belongings, moving between rooms, and — for older pupils — managing their own timetable and any medical routines.",
  "These are adaptive skills. Adaptive behaviour measures such as the ABAS-3 (Harrison & Oakland, 2015) and Vineland-3 (Sparrow et al., 2016) include self-care and daily living domains, and adaptive functioning is part of how intellectual disability is defined in DSM-5-TR.",
  "Summers, Larkin and Dewey (2008) found children with DCD had difficulty with dressing, personal hygiene and eating skills and took longer to master them — self-care difficulty is often motor as well as cognitive.",
  "Independence is shaped by the SUPPORT AROUND the child as well as their skills: Giangreco et al. (1997) described how close, constant adult proximity can reduce independence and peer interaction. Part D lists this as an added descriptive presentation under 1.6.",
 ],
 "what_it_is_not": [
  "NOT the same at home and at school. A child who dresses independently at home with unlimited time may not manage in a crowded changing room in five minutes — check both settings before concluding.",
  "NOT solved by more adult help. Doing the task for the child is quicker today and builds dependence over the year; the goal is planned fading.",
  "NOT only a special-education issue. Mainstream pupils with DCD, autism or hypermobility commonly have self-care needs that are invisible until PE, lunch or a school trip.",
 ],
 "by_age": [
  "EARLY YEARS 0–5: toileting, coat and shoes, eating, tidying — the core of preschool independence. Wide normal variation; describe relative to the setting and involve parents in shared targets.",
  "SCHOOL AGE 6–12: PE changing, laces and buttons, lunch boxes, bringing the right books, managing the toilet independently, following the day's routine without prompts.",
  "ADOLESCENT 13–16: lockers, timetables, moving between rooms, managing money in the canteen, personal hygiene and menstrual care, self-management of medical needs.",
  "YOUNG ADULT 17–26: travel, cooking, money, self-advocacy; transition planning to further education, training or work — adaptive measures at adult level (ABAS-3 adult form, Vineland-3 adult) inform this.",
  "SPECIAL SETTING: independence targets are central to the curriculum; check whether skills taught in school are practised at home and vice versa.",
 ],
 "assess": [
  "Observation at the points of greatest demand — PE changing, lunch, home time, moving between rooms — timing the task and noting what prompts are given and by whom.",
  "ABAS-3 or Vineland-3 (parent and teacher) where a broader adaptive profile is needed, e.g., when intellectual disability or transition planning is in question.",
  "Task analysis of the specific skill: which steps the child does alone, with a prompt, or not at all — this is the baseline for teaching.",
  "Map adult support: how much help the SNA or teacher gives, whether it has faded over time, and what the child does when the adult is not there.",
 ],
 "recommendations": [
  "TEACH ONE SKILL AT A TIME USING TASK ANALYSIS: break it into steps; use backward chaining (the adult does all but the last step, the child completes it, then the last two...) so the child always finishes successfully.",
  "VISUAL SEQUENCES: photographs or symbols of each step at the point of use (changing area, toilet, locker).",
  "PLANNED PROMPT FADING: move from physical to gestural to verbal to no prompt; write the fading plan into the Student Support Plan with dates. Review the SNA role against independence goals — access to the SNA scheme is for care needs (check the current Department of Education circular and NCSE guidance).",
  "ADAPT THE TASK: velcro or elastic laces, uniform with easier fastenings, lunch in easy-open containers, extra time or an earlier start for changing.",
  "HOME–SCHOOL LINK: agree the same method and cue words at home, and practise at weekends when time pressure is low.",
  "CONTINUUM LEVEL: Classroom Support to School Support; School Support Plus where OT, CDNT or physiotherapy are involved. REFER to OT (Primary Care or CDNT) for motor-based self-care difficulty.",
 ],
 "explain_parent": [
  "'He can do more than he's getting a chance to show — the adults around him have been helping to save time. We're going to plan how to step back gradually.'",
  "'We'll teach one skill at a time, starting at the end — you do all of the zip except pulling it to the top, and he does that. Then a bit more each week.'",
  "'Using the same words and the same order at home and school makes it much faster.'",
 ],
 "explain_teacher": [
  "'She dresses herself at home — just slowly. Give her a five-minute head start before PE and see what she can do alone.'",
  "'Can we write down what help the SNA gives for toileting and lunch, and plan to reduce it one step at a time? Otherwise it never reduces.'",
 ],
 "explain_child": [
  "YOUNGER: 'You're getting bigger and able to do more things yourself. Let's pick one thing to practise — which one would you like?'",
  "OLDER: 'The goal is for you to be able to do this without anyone hovering. What would you like to be able to do on your own by the summer?'",
  "ASK: 'Is there anything at school you'd like help with, or would like less help with?' — older pupils often want less adult help than they receive.",
 ],
 "red_flags": [
  "WATCH — new wetting or soiling after being continent, or regression in self-care: GP review, and consider emotional causes; in some cases this can be an indicator of distress or abuse — follow Children First (2017) and the school's child protection procedures; report to Tusla as soon as practicable where the threshold is met.",
  "WATCH — persistent poor hygiene, hunger or unsuitable clothing: consider neglect under Children First (2017) guidance, while distinguishing poverty from neglect; inform the DLP and, as a mandated person, report to Tusla as soon as practicable where the threshold is met — telling the DLP does not discharge your duty.",
  "BOUNDARY — intimate care must follow the school's intimate care policy; do not recommend arrangements that bypass it.",
 ],
 "questions": [
  "Q: 'Isn't it quicker if the SNA just does it?' A: 'Today, yes. Over a year, it makes him more dependent and more noticeable. A planned step back takes a little longer now and saves a lot later.'",
  "Q: 'Will he lose his SNA support if he becomes more independent?' A: 'SNA allocation is decided by the NCSE under current criteria, not by me. What matters for him is being as independent as he can be.'",
  "Q: 'Why can she do it at home but not in school?' A: 'Time, noise and an audience. We'll give her more time and a quieter space first, then build up.'",
 ],
 "supervision": [
  "Bring a task analysis and prompt-fading plan you have written — is it specific enough for the SNA to follow and review?",
  "Discuss how to raise over-support sensitively with staff who are working hard to help.",
 ],
 "citations": [
  "Giangreco, M. F., Edelman, S. W., Luiselli, T. E., & MacFarland, S. Z. C. (1997). Helping or hovering? Effects of instructional assistant proximity on students with disabilities. Exceptional Children, 64(1), 7–18.",
  "Harrison, P. L., & Oakland, T. (2015). Adaptive Behavior Assessment System (3rd ed.). Western Psychological Services.",
  "Sparrow, S. S., Cicchetti, D. V., & Saulnier, C. A. (2016). Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (3rd ed.). Pearson.",
  "Summers, J., Larkin, D., & Dewey, D. (2008). Activities of daily living in children with developmental coordination disorder: Dressing, personal hygiene, and eating skills. Human Movement Science, 27(2), 215–229.",
  "National Council for Special Education. (2018). Comprehensive review of the Special Needs Assistant scheme: A new school inclusion model to deliver the right supports at the right time to students with additional care needs. NCSE.",
 ],
})

